E
corners~
Sand collects in the corners.
~
E
chamber campsite site~
   The chamber isn't very large, but there's enough space that someone has set
up a campsite.
~
E
archways arches~
Four archways exit this chamber, though one is bricked up.
~
E
floor~
   The floor, like the walls, is made from enormous, well-fitted stone blocks
with no gaps or mortar at the seams. Sand is strewn here and there, and
collects in the corners.
~
E
rope~
   An old rope is crumpled on the floor, overtaken by mushrooms and too fragile
to move. It's not doing much good down here, anyway.
~
E
north east south west northeast ne southeast se southwest sw northwest nw~
It's hard to tell which way to start. Any path could lead to the center.
~
E
stones walls~
   The labyrinthine walls are made of square and rectangular stones of all
sizes, arranged like a puzzle. Each stone is cut to match its neighbor so
precisely that there are no gaps between them; no mortar was used to create
this cyclopean maze.
   There is, however, one wall that is made from ordinary brick.
~
E
bricks clay mortar~
   There once existed a fourth exit from this chamber, apparently, but it has
been walled over with clay bricks. Unlike the stone walls, these bricks are
held together by ordinary mortar.
~
E
chalk marks~
   Chalk marks on the stones around each path look like they might once have
been a map -- or, at least, a warning. But they've been rubbed out and
re-marked so many times, you can't make out the original intent.
~
E
sand~
   A fine layer of sand covers parts of the floor and collects in small piles
along the edges and corners.
~
