E
chamber beehive~
   It's a rather large chamber compared to the rest of the labyrinth, rising
upward in a beehive shape, coming together near the top where the ladder exits.
~
E
stone blocks~
   The cyclopean chamber is entirely made from enormous stone blocks, each one
perfectly shaped to come together like a puzzle. Alcoves cover the higher
walls, each with a statue in it.
~
E
alcoves statues~
   The beehive-like chamber has many alcoves spiraling up toward the ceiling.
Many are filled with statues, notably including a winged horse, a winged bull,
a winged man, and a winged woman. All the lower statues have been destroyed,
however so the alcoves nearest the floor have little more than feet and ankles.
~
E
sand floor~
The floor is covered in fine arena sand.
~
E
stones walls~
   Like the rest of the labyrinth, this chamber is built with cyclopean
architecture: tremendously large blocks, many of them taller than you are, are
cut so exactly that they meet at a hairline seam with no mortar or binder. The
stones are dark.
   The lowest part of the wall has a series of archways, each one bricked over
long ago.
~
E
ladder~
   The ladder is metal -- perhaps bronze -- but age and water have left it
stained and encrusted. Still, it may be the only way out.
~
